WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.670
MODERATOR: Good afternoon, and
welcome to the Sixth Annual

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.770
Space Grand Public Lecture
sponsored by the Massachusetts

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.080
Space Grand Consortium.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.230
Our speaker this afternoon has
traveled further than anyone

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:32.980
has, and most likely will ever
travel, to give this lecture.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.997
Jack Schmitt went to
the Moon and back,

00:00:34.997 --> 00:00:37.080
and he's going to tell us
a little bit about that.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:43.990
Now, for the benefit of
those in the audience who

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.070
don't have any
gray in your hair,

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.810
put yourself back
into the mindset

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.570
of the American population
in the late '60s,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.540
and the excitement that
was generated throughout,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.510
not just in the MITs and the
Harvards and the Caltechs

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.080
and the Stanfords of the world,
but throughout the community

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.260
about the excitement
of preparing

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:11.040
for probably the boldest human
space exploration of all time.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.170
It, of course, reached
its initial excitement

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.060
in the footstep that everybody
remembers with Neil Armstrong

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.280
and our alumnus Buzz Aldrin.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.840
And it continued on through
a science gathering activity,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.140
which has, in many ways,
never been repeated

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.270
in the history of
the space program,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.780
culminating with
the first scientist

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.750
to walk on the Moon, our
speaker today, who was also--

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.260
and this is somewhat ironic
to look back upon it--

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.380
the last man to
walk upon the Moon.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.010
Jack Schmitt, who was a
good friend to many of us

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:54.500
here in Cambridge, began
work as a geologist,

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.150
and he had a very promising
career as a geologist.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.360
And when he was selected
by NASA as an astronaut

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.070
to train for the Apollo
program, and performed

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.699
the way many of us had always
hoped scientists and engineers

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:11.590
in space would
perform in the course

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.640
of his exciting
exploration of the Moon.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.080
Beyond that, after return,
and after some important work

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.890
with the space agency, he
went on to another challenge,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.580
in many ways just
as great, and that

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.760
is in attempting successfully,
to a great extent,

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.040
to have his ideas
carried forward

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.640
to fruition for the
benefit of all of us

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.880
as a member of the United States
Senate, a Senator from his home

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.050
state of New Mexico.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.720
And since that
period of time, Jack

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.940
has remained active
in a number of areas,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.943
including the space arena where
we continue to bump into him.

00:02:50.943 --> 00:02:52.360
I'd be happy to
say, we're usually

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.280
on the same side of the
vigorous arguments that ensue.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.510
And what we have asked Senator
Harrison Schmitt to do today

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.110
in his talk, A Trip To The
Moon, is talk about not only

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.840
what it was like,
but what it can be

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.430
like for the next generation.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.082
Senator Schmitt.

00:03:12.082 --> 00:03:16.066
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.170
SCHMITT: Well,
thank you very much,

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.480
Larry, and I appreciate
the introduction,

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.510
and I also appreciate
the opportunity

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.730
to speak once more at
MIT on this subject.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:32.060
I think the last time
was about 20 years ago

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.640
and it was to a roomful of
geologists and Earth scientists

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.320
from MIT and Harvard.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.605
So I don't think any of
those people are here,

00:03:38.605 --> 00:03:39.980
and so I'm probably
not repeating

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.020
anything I might
have said and I don't

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.988
have to worry about
being consistent.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:47.900
Larry's exactly right.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.530
What I would like to do is to
take you on a trip to the Moon.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.780
I'm not going to spend a lot
of time getting you back.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.990
Usually, for many
audiences, I do

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.760
spend some time
getting everybody back,

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.430
but we won't worry
about that today.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.660
Because I [AUDIO OUT] briefly
summarize the first order

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.700
understanding of
this small planet

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.190
that we gained as a consequence
of the Apollo program.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.600
An extremely important
consequence, and not one that

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.329
was anticipated by John F.
Kennedy when he challenged

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:25.560
the nation to go to the Moon
and return safely to Earth,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.050
but one which in the
process of implementation

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.260
of that challenge, we realized
a great deal could be done

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.810
and, indeed, a
great deal was done.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.508
Also, at various
points in all of this

00:04:40.508 --> 00:04:42.300
you'll hear me talk a
little bit about what

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.080
I think the future may
hold, not only for the Moon,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.580
but in its now increasingly
symbiotic relationship

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:55.200
with the Earth, and ultimately,
with human settlement

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.770
of the solar system in general.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.830
So with that brief
introduction, if we

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.190
could have the lights down.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:06.190
And the first slide,
that's up to me, I guess.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:06.930
Did it go?

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:12.740
There we go.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:14.800
And if the speaker
light could come down,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.112
I think the slides would--

00:05:16.112 --> 00:05:18.070
You've all seen me, so
you don't need to see me

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.140
through this whole thing.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.870
Can I control that, or could
we have the speaker light down?

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.610
Maybe we can't do that.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.230
At any rate, we
began these trips

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:39.760
to the Moon in the late
'60s and early 1970s

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.190
from the Kennedy Space Center.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.680
And here you see
the Saturn V rocket

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.490
to be used by the
Apollo 17 mission,

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:52.000
moving away from its assembly
building, the vehicle assembly

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.510
building, towards
the launch pad.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:59.800
It is still the
largest rocket that

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.040
has ever been used to put
human beings in space.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.860
364 feet high.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:10.270
The basic fully fuel weight
was 6.2 million pounds.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:15.010
And the five big engines
in the first stage

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.090
developed 7.5 million
pounds of thrust.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.130
Now, the Space Shuttle
approaches those numbers,

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.590
but does not reach them.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.240
And it was, indeed, exactly
what we needed for this mission.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.710
A truly remarkable
accomplishment

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.040
is putting this system
together, and one

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.060
which worked with extraordinary
reliability each time

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.700
we demanded something of it.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.720
By the way, that vehicle
assembly building is now

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.210
fully utilized, unlike
in the Apollo program,

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:51.070
fully utilized by the activities
of the Space Shuttle program.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.010
This is just an alternate
view of the Saturn

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.757
V looking from the bottom up.

00:06:55.757 --> 00:06:58.090
Those of you who can see in
the lower right-hand corner,

00:06:58.090 --> 00:06:59.520
the upper torso of a man.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.330
We'll get a feeling
for scale here.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.190
It's quite a large vehicle.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.790
That D on the fin, left-hand
fin is about five feet high,

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:09.670
I would suspect.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:14.710
Really awesome, not only from
the bottom, but from the top.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:18.880
Here, now looking across
the top and the launch

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.330
escape rocket that is
attached to our capsule,

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:26.830
that you can see there-- the
cone-shaped capsule at the top

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.620
of the rocket, looking back
towards that vehicle assembly

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.560
building that I
mentioned earlier.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.710
I generally don't need this, but
that's the building out there.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:39.160
We were on our way out to
the launch pad at this point,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.050
moving at less than
a mile per hour

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:47.290
on this huge machine
we call the crawler.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.970
By the way, the
crawlers are still

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.510
used to transport the space
shuttles out to the launch pad.

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.080
Now, we had many different
kinds of training.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.990
Thousands of hours, of course,
in computer-driven simulators

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:02.420
that prepared us for the actual
flight portions of our mission.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.470
But also, we had things
like survival training.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:07.540
And here you see some
very young and skeptical

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.970
looking astronauts
learning about the care,

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:16.270
feeding, preparation of a boa
constrictor at an Air Force

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.790
station in Panama.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:24.460
Ed Mitchell, MIT graduate,
is in that picture, a PhD.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.800
I don't know whether--

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:27.860
Charlie Duke, was he?

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:28.780
AUDIENCE: He was my student.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:29.363
SCHMITT: Yeah.

00:08:29.363 --> 00:08:32.422
Well Charlie's behind the boa.

00:08:32.422 --> 00:08:33.880
You might not be
able to tell that,

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.650
but I remember that
he was behind the boa.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.010
Now, we also had
science training.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:44.670
And as a consequence of
the efforts of many people

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.850
throughout the country,
including Bill Brace,

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.700
here at MIT, and some
Harvard professors

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.350
and some Caltech
field geologists,

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:56.790
we finally developed, for
the last three missions,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.310
a comprehensive Earth
science training

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.310
program that prepared
pilot astronauts about

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.720
as well as one I
think could ever

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.250
expect to prepare these, really,
already outstanding observers

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:11.310
to systematically
collect samples,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.400
establish their context
through verbal description

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.570
and photography, and to give us
the basis for that first order

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.600
understanding of a second
planet that I mentioned earlier.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.270
Here you see Gene Cernan, the
commander of the Apollo 17

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:28.210
mission, and yours truly in
an exercise near Flagstaff,

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.780
Arizona, a science exercise.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:33.060
We actually, each
time we went out

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.450
for the most part,
[AUDIO OUT] action profiles,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.880
but working on the
geology, the science,

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.920
of real Earth problems.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.570
Why Gene felt, in
this context, it

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:46.590
was necessary to wear
checkered pants I'm not sure.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:49.020
He was, and I guess
he would still

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.170
say is, a Navy test
pilot, and that might

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:54.450
explain the checkered pants.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:01.470
Gene also was the talker on
the crew, believe it or not.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:03.780
And here you see
us in an exercise

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.950
involving our actual flight
lunar roving vehicle.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.750
We were checking
out all the, what

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.970
they call the fit and function
of it in our pressure suits,

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.420
and Gene had come up with
something that excited him,

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.070
and it obviously
put me to sleep.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:24.880
But ultimately, on
December 7th, Florida time,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:30.900
in 1972 we left the Earth with
a spectacular night launch,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.550
I am told.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:34.770
Having just seen
Discovery launch at night,

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.900
I can get some feeling
now for what it was like.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.860
But, of course, I missed that.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.530
And inside the capsule, we were
subjected to, at this point,

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:47.010
not very heavy Gs, because
the initial acceleration

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.860
is quite slow, but
extraordinarily heavy

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.280
vibration.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.250
In fact, the instrument panel,
switch panel in front of me

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.770
as I was lying on my back,
was vibrating so much

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.270
that I could not read the dial.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.940
So it was always some
question in my mind,

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.300
why did I go through all those
launch abort simulations when

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.790
I wasn't going to be
able to read the dials

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.765
during that period of time.

00:11:10.765 --> 00:11:12.390
Now, on the other
hand, had we actually

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.640
had to abort for
some serious problem,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.670
I'm sure my adrenaline would
have focused my eyes as

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.190
rapidly as the
panel was vibrating

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:24.180
and everything would have
worked out just fine.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.090
It actually took 14
seconds to reach a point

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.060
about like this, go the
length of the rocket.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:34.380
It took two minutes and 45
seconds to get to a full

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:39.390
acceleration on the first
stage, which was about four g's.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.090
That was the
greatest acceleration

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.370
we had during the launch.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.170
But at that point,
we, of course,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:51.480
had reached the limit of fuel
in the first stage rocket.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.970
We turned it off, staged--

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:56.130
that is, dropped it
off into the Atlantic--

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.430
and then reignited the
second stage rocket

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:02.750
in a sequence that went
from four g's to a minus one

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.160
and 1/2 g's, as that
whole stack actually

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.880
unloaded a little bit, to
a plus one and 1/2 g's,

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.850
and all of that happened
in just over a second.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.730
That gets your attention.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.250
The checklists bang
back and forth,

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:20.757
and you know why you're
strapped into your couch,

00:12:20.757 --> 00:12:22.590
because otherwise, you
would be banging back

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.730
and forth at the same time.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:28.610
But after we went
through the second stage

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.410
and ignited a third stage,
which was actually restartable,

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:34.310
because we will see that
we're going to use it again,

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:35.850
the S-IVB.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:39.150
We were in orbit
after, as I recall,

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.970
a little under 10
minutes, and had a chance

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.750
to briefly look at
some spectacular views

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:46.950
of this planet Earth.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:50.190
Here, the South Atlantic
and its cloud patterns,

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.830
the Coast of Africa
and the sand dunes,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.890
and on over the Kalahari
Desert looking down at these

00:12:55.890 --> 00:13:01.440
frost-like patterns, actually
turn out to be burn scars--

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.440
apparently, fires set
by the local inhabitants

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:09.270
to improve the grazing
for the next season.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:12.290
Over the Indian Ocean and
the magnificent thunderheads

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:14.310
that one can see
there almost any time,

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:18.240
particularly at the
Intertropical Convergence Zone.

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.490
And then to a sunset--

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:21.810
oh, actually, I'm sorry.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.660
These are the atolls of
the Great Barrier Reef,

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:27.990
I believe, near Australia.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.670
And the turquoise
blue, by the way,

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:34.770
shows up extremely distinctly
from way, way out into space.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:39.460
Quite a remarkable contrast with
the deeper blue of the oceans.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:41.370
It took 90 minutes to
go around the Earth.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.760
We were traveling at about
18,000 miles an hour.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.890
We'd made two trips
around the Earth

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.380
to make sure all the
spacecraft and rocket systems

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.870
were ready to go to the
Moon, and indeed they were.

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:54.510
And so at the end
of our second trip,

00:13:54.510 --> 00:14:01.560
and after passing through a
sunset, I believe in this case,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.340
we ignited the third
stage rocket, the S-IVB,

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:08.550
began to accelerate over
about a five minute period

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.890
to 25,000 miles an hour.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:13.260
So through a sunrise--

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:15.840
the most spectacular
sunrise I've ever seen--

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.600
until we were, indeed,
on our way to the Moon

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.170
in nearly perfect trajectory.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:26.070
At that point, we undocked the
main spacecraft, the command

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.300
and service module--

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.280
America was its name--

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.850
from the S-IVB rocket,
moved away a little bit,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.980
turned around, and then
moved back in in order

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.010
to extract the lunar module,
Challenger, which you see here

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.090
tucked away and folded it
up in the end of the rocket.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.630
This is the docking
port and tunnel.

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:47.850
We retract that so we all
could go to the Moon together.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.510
Now, those bright spots that
you see around the S-IVB

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:52.770
are pieces of ice.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:56.730
Actually, ice that had condensed
from the atmosphere in Florida

00:14:56.730 --> 00:15:01.140
on the cold fuel and
oxidizer tanks of the S-IVB,

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:06.450
and came off when we shut down
the engine for the last time.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.960
Those are probably what my good
friend and former colleague

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:14.520
John Glenn thought were
fireflies over Australia.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.820
Now he is the Democratic
Senator from the State of Ohio,

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.340
and that might explain
that little anomaly.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.920
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:25.590
The S-IVB sideway,
just so you can

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.990
see what it looked
like as it coasted

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.930
with us at 25,000 miles
an hour initially,

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.190
to an impact on the Moon.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:35.100
Actually, this became a science
experiment at this point,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:38.130
because we already had
in place a seismic net

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.310
from the previous
missions, and the S-IVB

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:43.320
was a source of
seismic energy, which

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.760
we directed in an appropriate
location for science

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.905
and seismology on the Moon.

00:15:50.905 --> 00:15:51.780
I can't remember now.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:54.810
I think it impacted after
we had gone behind the Moon,

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.320
as you'll see in a few moments.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.570
But once we had the
Challenger in tow,

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:05.730
we were able to look back at
this beautiful planet rapidly

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:07.170
receding from us.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.040
Here, still filling the
window, but eventually not even

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:14.340
doing that, and we were
able to take this very, very

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.700
popular picture of
our dear planet Earth,

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:21.360
showing the entire continent
of Africa, most of Antarctica.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:27.480
A hurricane, our typhoon,
going ashore here on India,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.040
on the subcontinent of India.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:35.820
Mediterranean there,
and the fronts

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.310
that are circling around
Antarctica very nicely shown.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.485
Really, a spectacular picture.

00:16:40.485 --> 00:16:41.610
We're all very proud of it.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:47.010
It is still the most requested
image from the NASA archives,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.390
as you might expect.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.450
The worst use of this
picture that I have ever seen

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.710
was in a-- and I'm even going
to mention the name-- a TWA

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:57.990
in-flight magazine where it
wasn't good enough, like this,

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.329
and so they moved
Australia over here.

00:17:00.329 --> 00:17:03.203
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:06.720
There is Australia.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:10.349
This was taken about
a day and a half

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:12.510
after the picture
I just showed you.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:15.359
We were in now, a lunar
reference trajectory,

00:17:15.359 --> 00:17:17.849
as it was called.

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:20.970
And that the Earth was
turning beneath us, once

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.630
every 24 hours, and going
through a slow phase

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:27.869
transition, as you see
here, not nearly as full.

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:30.180
And here, looking at
the South Pacific,

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.720
you see how brilliantly
Australia, as a desert beacon,

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:34.320
shows up.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.780
And indeed, you could see
the deserts and particularly

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:40.950
Australia very clearly from
the Moon and identify them.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.290
A number of things.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:46.080
Being an amateur meteorologist,
I filled the transcripts

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.320
with all sorts of
descriptions and forecasts

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:51.750
as we went towards the Moon
for three and 1/2 days.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:54.000
I'm sure I put everybody in
the mission control center

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.040
to sleep, but I enjoyed myself.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:00.120
There wasn't much else to do,
as you travel out towards space,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.290
except make sure you
didn't get into trouble.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.830
This is a typhoon in
the Philippine Sea,

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.980
and those spots right there
are thunderheads on each

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:10.200
of the Philippine islands.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:13.090
Another typhoon was
developing around Guam,

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:14.700
which nobody knew
about, and we were

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.410
able to tell the world about.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.480
And particularly the
Air Force was interested

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.480
because they had
recovery airplane flying

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.900
in that area in
support of our mission.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:28.920
This speckle point is an
interesting phenomenon,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.630
and actually is a
way in which visually

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:32.700
you can tell what
the sea state is.

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:35.730
It's always brighter,
for example,

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:39.260
near continental shores
because of the increased wave

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:42.350
activity at that point.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.330
If you look carefully,
that's China up there,

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.520
and Southeast Asia.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.760
This front has
just cleared Japan.

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:54.200
Japan's not quite so clear
and obvious at this point.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:56.160
And the Earth
continues to recede.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:00.980
About three days out, we had
another view of Australia,

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:03.920
but at this point, we
became increasingly aware

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:07.820
of a dark looming shape
blocking out the star field,

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:13.790
as well as the Earth itself
when we headed towards orbit.

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:15.800
And there you see the
mountains of the Moon

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:17.660
across our dear
planet Earth again.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:24.650
The first sunrise that we had in
the spacecraft behind the Moon

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:26.300
is shown here.

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:29.060
Actually, it's a
sunset on the Moon.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:31.010
We were in a
retrograde orbit, so

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:34.460
that when we landed on the
other side of the Moon,

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:36.170
in the valley of
Taurus-Littrow, we

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:38.030
would have the sun to our back.

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:40.490
It made just for better
visibility, of course,

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:43.220
than flying into the sun itself.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.820
This illustrates a number
of properties of the Moon

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.470
that are of interest.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.480
For one thing, you see
absolutely no evidence

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:52.580
of an atmosphere.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:55.280
Off there on the
horizon, the vacuum

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:59.240
at the surface of the Moon is
about 10 to the minus 12 Torr.

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.690
Basically, no
atmospheric gases at all,

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:06.190
although there is a very
small and measurable amount

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:06.980
of atmosphere.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:11.520
There's no oxidation
of iron minerals,

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.700
so you get none of
the colorful features

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:15.290
that we're used
to here on Earth.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:17.340
But you do have a
beauty of contrast,

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.690
which this picture and others I
think will help to illustrate.

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:22.157
Now, the reason I
show this picture

00:20:22.157 --> 00:20:23.240
somewhat out of sequence--

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.550
I actually took
this on the way home

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.770
just after we left
lunar orbit-- but it's

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:30.740
to give you an indication
of where we were going.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.300
The valley of
Taurus-Littrow, which

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:37.190
you'll see more of in a moment,
cut through a radial valley,

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:40.580
cutting through the ring
of mountains surrounding

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:46.400
this relatively young circular
basin called Serenitatis.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.670
Serenitatis is 500
kilometers in diameter,

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:52.700
somewhat bigger than
Crisium, which you see here,

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:55.220
but not quite as big as
Tranquillitatis, which is

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:58.010
the more irregular basin here.

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:00.440
Apollo 11, Neil Armstrong
and Buzz Aldrin,

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:01.310
landed right there.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:05.150
That Tranquility Base is in
that vicinity of the Moon.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:07.700
Our landing, as I say, was
somewhat North of that.

00:21:10.226 --> 00:21:14.550
A better view, a close-up view
of the Valley of Taurus-Littrow

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:15.780
is shown here.

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:17.870
It's that dark bay-like
indentation here.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:23.330
It's about 35 miles long,
about four and 1/2 miles wide.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.640
And those mountains
on either side,

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.750
although seemingly
somewhat subdued,

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:34.250
rise to 7,000 feet at
their highest points.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:39.050
So we landed in this valley,
or a point about right there,

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:43.370
in a valley deeper than the
Grand Canyon of the Colorado.

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:46.220
Truly a remarkable
demonstration, once again,

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:48.350
of the navigational
techniques that

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:54.050
had been developed by the Apollo
program, and in particular,

00:21:54.050 --> 00:22:01.220
with the assistance and
innovation and imagination

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:04.280
of the Draper Laboratory, which
then was the MIT Instrument

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.990
Lab.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:07.160
Had they been spun off yet?

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:08.960
I've forgotten when
they were spun off.

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:09.770
Somebody tell me.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:10.250
AUDIENCE: It was
after [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:12.375
SCHMITT: It was after that
when they were spun off.

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:17.540
I am privileged to serve on the
Board of the Draper Lab now.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:22.055
So I get to come back and
thank them as many times

00:22:22.055 --> 00:22:23.180
as I can remember to do it.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:28.860
This picture was taken
from about 100 miles away.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:32.420
This one from about 15 miles
away and in a somewhat lower

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.610
altitude.

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:36.860
We were only about
10 nautical miles

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:39.200
above the surface at this point.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:41.350
The landing site is
going to be there.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:44.510
This spot right here is
the other spacecraft.

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:47.840
Ron Evans, who was the
third man on our crew,

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.180
was alone at this point,
since this picture

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:53.330
was taken from the lunar
module, as we prepared

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:56.540
for our last orbit and
descent to the Valley

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:57.810
of Taurus-Littrow.

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.440
Ron was about a mile
ahead and a little bit

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:04.610
below us preparing to get
out of the way of our descent

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:06.260
on the next orbit.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:09.070
This mountain here is
the highest mountain

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.880
that borders the valley.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:17.100
Again, something
over 7,000 feet high.

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:20.970
Looking straight down on
the valley is of interest.

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:24.450
We landed right there, and those
of you who are close enough

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:26.550
will see a dark
shadow or spot there.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:28.680
That is the shadow
of the lunar module.

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:33.090
Ron actually took this picture
as he passed overhead while we

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.120
were working on the surface.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:38.520
There is a light-colored
spot around this area here

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.220
that was caused by the descent
effluence just altering

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.450
the optical properties
of the surface.

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:49.230
We've never been able to
detect any impregnation

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:51.680
of the effluence of
the decent engine,

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:55.650
just a modification of
the surface texture.

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:58.080
This feature here, this
plume-like deposit,

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:02.400
is the remains of an
avalanche that came off

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.100
the side of the South Massif,
that high mountain I pointed

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.360
out earlier.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:09.090
Very probably in
response to material

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:13.620
thrown over 2,000 kilometers
from the Crater Tycho

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:15.660
in the southern
part of the Moon.

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:18.170
Those of you who look at
the Moon once in a while

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:19.510
will know that name.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:22.950
It's one of the younger bright
halo craters, bright ray

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:24.300
craters on the Moon.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:26.400
And it appears as
if one of its rays

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:28.740
comes all the way up here
across Taurus-Littrow,

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:32.460
and we are suspecting
that that avalanche was

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:37.410
triggered by the impact
of that secondary debris.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:40.950
The ages, as far as we
can tell the age of Tycho,

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.820
and the age of this,
about 30 million years,

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:46.070
work out to be
approximately right.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:48.540
So that's the present
working hypothesis

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:52.320
that we, indeed, had evidence,
or worked on an avalanche

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:55.630
caused by Tycho.

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:58.740
Our traverses, the
first excursion

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:02.920
we worked primarily around
the lunar module deploying

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:08.400
the Apollo Science Package, the
ALSEP, about 300 meters ahead

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:11.310
of the Challenger.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.690
We also sampled around
craters in this area.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.620
The second excursion,
though, we got going early,

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.350
we headed all the way out
here about seven kilometers

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:21.660
to that point right there,
and worked our way over

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:25.320
to this dark crater where
we found the orange soil,

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.330
and then back to
the lunar module.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:28.920
And the third
excursion was over here

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:31.020
to visit some big boulders
you'll see in a moment.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.150
Fact is, I think
it's right there.

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:34.680
And then back to there.

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:37.560
So we traveled
about 35 kilometers

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:40.410
in the lunar Rover, which
I'll show you in a moment,

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:44.790
and collected about
250 pounds of samples,

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:47.310
several thousand photographs,
and generally had,

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:51.060
not only a great, enjoyable
time, but a fairly productive

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:53.730
scientific mission as well.

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.970
This is the first view I had
out the right-hand window

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.590
of the Challenger after landing.

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:02.400
It's a view over a
fairly typical dark, mare

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:03.480
surface of the Moon.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.450
The scattered blocks of
basalt, and the ground up

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:08.460
and pulverized
basalt that makes up

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.640
what we call the lunar regolith,
that debris layer, or sometimes

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:14.040
called soil layer,
that covers the Moon.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:19.140
In this valley it averages
about nine meters thick.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:22.350
The regolith of the
Moon is the source

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:24.690
of many potential
resources we'll talk about

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:29.240
in a few moments, including
an energy resource,

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:32.010
a light isotope of
helium called Helium 3

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:34.650
that is a nearly ideal,
environmentally, as well

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:40.370
as efficiency, ideal
fuel for nuclear fusion.

00:26:40.370 --> 00:26:45.210
The metallic object
there in the foreground

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.360
is one of 16 small
restartable rockets

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:51.090
that we used to control the
attitude of the spacecraft

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:53.430
when we were in flight.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:57.020
The far edge of
the valley there,

00:26:57.020 --> 00:26:59.970
say that point right in there,
is probably about four miles

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.350
away, something like that.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:04.080
Now, you have to take my word
for these distances and sizes

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.600
and things like that, because
there are no telephone

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:08.640
poles or trees or
houses or anything

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.920
that you can use for reference.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:14.430
And indeed, astronauts,
almost invariably,

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:20.820
underestimated the
distances, like sometimes you

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:22.800
do when you drive in the
Western United States.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:24.690
When the air is
clear and they have

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:28.040
no clear reference of size,
you will do the same thing.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:36.710
The Challenger, our home
away from home on the Moon.

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:38.560
Really an excellent camp.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:40.658
Not only did the descent
stage, the lower half

00:27:40.658 --> 00:27:42.200
of the Challenger,
contain everything

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:45.570
that we could need for
our particular mission,

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.720
including the lunar Rover
and the Science Packages,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:49.760
and even our food and film.

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:54.590
The ascent stage was
really a very good camp.

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:57.050
Sleeping in one-sixth gravity
that you have on the Moon

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:58.760
is very comfortable.

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:03.110
We had hammocks that
provided very good rest.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:05.180
Not enough gravity
to make you want

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.380
to toss and turn,
but just enough

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:09.380
to feel like you were
lying on something, rather

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:10.850
than floating.

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:12.950
The food was reasonably good.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:15.860
Certainly better than I've
had in some of my field camps

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:19.820
that I've worked in
as a field geologist.

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:20.840
No hot water.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.600
We had hot water in the other
spacecraft, the America,

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:27.410
but we did not have hot
water in the lunar module.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:30.410
That just was not provided
for, and it was not

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:34.700
really necessary for
three days on the Moon.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:36.920
And maybe most
important for geologists

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:39.590
and maybe some others, there
are no black flies or mosquitoes

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:40.173
to bother you.

00:28:40.173 --> 00:28:44.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:48.430
This picture was taken
from about 75 meters away.

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:52.010
Now, you may have
already wondered

00:28:52.010 --> 00:28:53.200
did we ever feel homesick.

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:56.110
Well, my answer is no, because
anytime I wanted to see home,

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:58.070
I could just look up over the--

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.950
here, the lunar
module, or the nearest

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:04.720
mountain and see the Earth
only 250,000 miles away.

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:07.690
Communications were
excellent, just outstanding.

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:13.660
We never had any communication
problems of any significance

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:14.750
whatsoever.

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:18.970
And we worked almost as if we
were working anywhere else you

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:20.790
could imagine here on Earth.

00:29:25.030 --> 00:29:28.780
A well-dressed astronaut
wears what you see here.

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:33.880
The space suit, the backpack,
the emergency oxygen

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:40.150
supply there, and the
man inside weighed,

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:45.790
in this case, about 370
pounds, Earth pounds,

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:50.230
but on the Moon that would
only be about 61 pounds.

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:52.630
Mass didn't change, of
course, but the weight

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:53.540
was quite different.

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:57.100
And so it was really
quite easy to move around.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.410
You had to watch
yourself a little bit.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.120
Your center of
gravity was displaced

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:03.790
about two inches
towards the back because

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:06.380
of the weight of the backpack.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:09.550
You'll see us leaning forward
in order to compensate for that.

00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:11.200
That was very natural to do.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:15.070
Although, Charlie Duke, when he
was on the Moon on Apollo 16,

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:18.640
decided to demonstrate what it
was like to jump straight up,

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:21.200
and he forgot that his center
of gravity was displaced,

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:24.310
and so he rotated out of
the view of the camera,

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:27.220
and everybody got a pretty
good laugh out of that.

00:30:31.159 --> 00:30:33.915
If you ever wonder why
astronauts are whistling

00:30:33.915 --> 00:30:36.290
and singing quite a bit while
they're working on the Moon

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:40.250
is because it's so much easier
than training in one gravity.

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:42.620
It really is.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:45.800
We were basically pressurized
for almost 10 hours

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:49.760
for each excursion, each
of the three excursions.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:55.910
And the only muscles that
really became fatigued

00:30:55.910 --> 00:31:00.140
were your forearm muscles
and the hand muscles.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:03.860
And that's because the glove was
just purely a pressure glove,

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:06.140
and every time you
wanted to move,

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:10.160
you had to squeeze against
that pressure, 3.5 PSI.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.917
And that would be like squeezing
a tennis ball continuously.

00:31:13.917 --> 00:31:15.500
Every time you wanted
to do something,

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:18.290
you had to squeeze a
tennis ball to do it.

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:21.800
That fatigue, these
forearm muscles,

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:24.950
no matter what you had done to
prepare yourself physically,

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:26.450
there was just
nothing you could do.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:29.930
After about a half hour
or so, you would finally

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:33.530
realize what your steady
state capability was,

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:34.740
and you'd proceed from there.

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:37.310
But the overall loss of
efficiency as a consequence

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:38.900
of that was significant.

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:42.950
And I hope that someday,
maybe some engineer from MIT

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:46.160
will figure out how to
engineer a glove that

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:49.610
more closely duplicates
the normal dexterity

00:31:49.610 --> 00:31:50.600
of the human hand.

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:52.890
That's one reason why we
put human beings in space

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:56.870
is to have that hand attached
to a supercomputer called

00:31:56.870 --> 00:32:00.260
the brain, and something
that we haven't yet

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:03.590
been able to duplicate
any other way.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:07.070
The rake that you see there
was just one of the many tools

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:09.890
that we had for collecting
a particular style

00:32:09.890 --> 00:32:14.390
of sample, small samples
out of this lunar regolith.

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:16.580
But you also see I'm
not going to the trouble

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:19.270
to grasp that rake, I just
rest it against my hand.

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:22.430
Again, anything you can do to
keep from using your forearm

00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:25.580
muscles you would do.

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:29.390
Gene Cernan is testing out
the lunar Rover, in this case.

00:32:29.390 --> 00:32:31.400
It weighed about
450 Earth pounds

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:34.550
without us or
anything else on it.

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:35.630
It worked extremely well.

00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:38.900
Had four wheel independent
drive, four wheel independent

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:40.340
suspension.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:42.620
It had front and
rear wheel steering.

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:46.280
You could do either direct or
Ackermann steering with it.

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:48.320
It just worked remarkably well.

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:50.750
Had no problems with the
dust because all the joints

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:53.150
and bearings were
sealed hermetically,

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:56.140
so the dust never was able
to penetrate into them.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:58.340
Served us extraordinarily
well on all three

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:00.088
of the missions
that it was used on.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:07.383
Getting on the lunar Rover was
not particularly difficult,

00:33:07.383 --> 00:33:08.550
once you learned the tricks.

00:33:08.550 --> 00:33:11.210
You stand at the side,
jump up into the vacuum

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:12.960
with a little bit of
leftward translation,

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:16.740
and hope that you came down
on the seat assigned to you.

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:18.210
Commanders tend
to get upset when

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:19.688
you come down in their seat.

00:33:19.688 --> 00:33:21.480
It's a little bit like
professors, I guess,

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:24.090
if they find you in
their chair somewhere.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:27.750
They're paid to get upset, so
we don't worry much about that.

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:30.130
One thing that lunar module
pilots are paid to do,

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:33.360
though, is to repair the
fenders broken by commanders.

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:36.460
On each of our Rover
missions, the commander

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:38.760
succeeded in breaking
a fender off,

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:40.470
and lunar module
pilots very quickly

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:42.080
were instructed on
how to repair it.

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:45.600
Here you see unneeded
photo maps taped together

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:48.040
with, you guessed
it, gray duct tape.

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:51.450
It held the Rover together,
just like it's probably holding

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:53.940
this building together by now.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:55.097
And it worked very well.

00:33:55.097 --> 00:33:57.180
And the reason this was
important-- it wasn't just

00:33:57.180 --> 00:34:00.360
a trivial exercise-- is
that in one sixth gravity,

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:02.280
this debris, when
you're driving,

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:04.020
would travel just far
enough up the wheel

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:05.940
to give you a
forward rooster tail,

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:08.610
and you'd be driving
into your own dust

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:11.496
if you did not have something
to break that trajectory.

00:34:14.605 --> 00:34:15.980
This picture you
have to look at,

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:17.940
because I like to look at it.

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:19.315
I never tire of it.

00:34:19.315 --> 00:34:22.550
It not only brings
back the memories

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:25.150
of the desert-like quality of
the Valley of Tarsus-Littrow

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:27.260
or any place on
the Moon, really,

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:34.100
but it illustrates
just the general nature

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:35.330
of what we were doing.

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:38.830
The lunar Rover off in
the middle distance,

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.880
the basalt boulders
around a crater

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:44.449
that's actually on the rim of a
crater 600 meters in diameter,

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:46.280
the largest in the
immediate area.

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:48.770
Probably a crater
formed by those Tycho

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:50.210
secondaries-- we're
just not sure.

00:34:57.110 --> 00:34:59.870
Now we're going to stand on
the North side of the valley.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:03.050
The next few pictures
will be a rough panorama

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:07.700
looking from the East
here towards the South.

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:10.520
This actually looks
along the path

00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:12.920
we flew to come into the valley.

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:16.150
This picture over the top
of a very large boulder,

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:19.340
with yours truly
for scale, actually

00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:20.650
includes the lunar module.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:22.400
That spot right there
is the lunar module,

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:24.290
about three and 1/2
miles away with that,

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:27.740
again, lightened
patch of material

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:32.030
at the top of the regolith,
quite visible at this point.

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:35.720
These boulders are made
up of fragments of rock.

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:36.730
They're called breccias.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:40.130
That's a term that geologists
use for rocks made up

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:42.390
of fragments of other rocks.

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:44.180
And it actually,
almost certainly,

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:46.550
formed as a result of
the huge impact that

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:52.820
created the Serenitatis Basin,
at whose edge we had landed.

00:35:52.820 --> 00:35:56.270
And it had rolled down the
mountain from about a kilometer

00:35:56.270 --> 00:35:59.600
and a half away, down
about a 26 degree slope

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.260
until it hit a break in slope.

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:03.330
Here, we're on about
a 20 degree slope.

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:04.790
And it broke into five pieces.

00:36:04.790 --> 00:36:08.980
But you can see here
that the near edge

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:11.940
of the trench, the boulder
track, if you will,

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:14.060
that it made as it
rolled to a stop,

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:17.060
my footprints are
coming down there out

00:36:17.060 --> 00:36:18.470
of sight and up the other side.

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:20.930
Those footprints will
stay in recognizable form

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:22.935
for about a million years.

00:36:22.935 --> 00:36:24.560
So if you want to
leave your footprints

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:27.890
in the sands of time, this
isn't a bad place to do it.

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:30.140
Certainly far more certain
than going to Washington.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:37.560
[LAUGHTER] View of
that South Massif,

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:40.290
the highest mountain
in the local area,

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:43.290
that 26 degree slope,
constant slope,

00:36:43.290 --> 00:36:45.538
built-in moguls
from small craters

00:36:45.538 --> 00:36:46.830
that are formed on the surface.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:50.190
I think it'd be a great
ski area one of these days.

00:36:50.190 --> 00:36:54.510
A 7,000 foot vertical
drop in one swell foop,

00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:55.770
that's not too bad.

00:36:59.970 --> 00:37:01.980
This is what things
looked like after we

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:04.050
had worked around the
lunar module for a while.

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:08.310
And the Rover is parked
there in its best

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:09.890
position for thermal control.

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:12.540
A lot of the thermal
control was passive.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:15.360
We tried to keep things as cool
as we possibly could, but not

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:18.060
too cold, because I
guess I should mention

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:20.910
that, say, a surface like
that in direct sunlight

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:25.890
would be probably at
200, 250 degrees C,

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:28.890
whereas in the shadow,
any shadow there,

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:30.330
the surface would
very quickly be

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:32.640
down at about a
minus 150 degrees

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:37.020
C. So tremendous temperature
contrast between the light

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:38.250
and shadow.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:41.670
Depending, of course, on the
absortivity/emissivity ratio

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:43.615
of the particular surface.

00:37:43.615 --> 00:37:45.240
But one reason for
showing this picture

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:48.180
is to illustrate the
nature of that debris

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:53.220
layer, the regolith, in which
our resources are contained.

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:55.200
And it's a lot like
damp beach sand.

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:58.687
It takes a very nice footprint
if you're careful with it.

00:37:58.687 --> 00:38:00.270
You can see these
show up very nicely.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.280
But you gradually
will stir it up.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:03.370
It's slightly cohesive.

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:05.370
When you scuff it, you
get a little bit of dust,

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:10.410
but you also get some clots
that spray out from your boot.

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:12.030
But gradually,
even though it has

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.220
a very high, in the
undisturbed area, out

00:38:14.220 --> 00:38:16.950
here it has a very
high bearing strength.

00:38:16.950 --> 00:38:19.080
No problem at all driving
or walking over it.

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:20.732
As you stir it up,
of course, it starts

00:38:20.732 --> 00:38:22.440
to lose that bearing
strength, and that's

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:25.500
an issue for those of you that
some day will design or live

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:26.340
in a lunar base.

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:32.310
You'll have to make
sure that somehow, you

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:34.800
create a surface
on the regolith,

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:39.180
possibly with just aggregate,
that doesn't end up being

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:40.980
a problem for the long term.

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:47.760
Gene took this picture
as we were preparing

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:51.360
to leave after our 75
hour stay, and 22 hours

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:53.310
outside the spacecraft--
both records,

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:56.610
I might presume to say.

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:59.250
Pilots like to keep records.

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:02.340
I'm reminded of that many times.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:05.010
You see yours truly
preparing to go up

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:07.510
the ladder with a
bag full of samples,

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:09.720
as any good geologist should.

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:13.140
And Gene, however,
was on his way

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:17.340
out to park the Rover so it
could watch our ascent back

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:20.240
into lunar orbit, which
if I'm not mistaken,

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:22.820
it will be the picture
after this one.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:25.080
This just is a picture
out the right-hand window

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.960
again, after we had
completed our adventures,

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.450
looking towards the
science station, which

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:34.560
is deployed out in that
area about 300 meters away.

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:38.110
The tracks of the Rover, and
yours truly running back,

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:40.110
or skiing, actually,
back and forth.

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:43.080
Cross-country ski technique
works very well on the Moon.

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:46.050
You can move at 10, maybe
even 15 kilometers an hour

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:49.260
if you work at it.

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:53.880
Stopping's another problem,
but by gliding just

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:57.360
over the surface rather than
on it, you do very well.

00:39:57.360 --> 00:40:01.300
The ascent is not
well photographed,

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:02.710
and I apologize for that.

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:07.337
This is taken off of
a television monitor.

00:40:07.337 --> 00:40:09.670
Gene tried to persuade me to
go outside and get a really

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:10.712
good picture of liftoff--

00:40:10.712 --> 00:40:12.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:15.190
--but I was developing some
political astuteness even

00:40:15.190 --> 00:40:17.260
at that point.

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:21.490
And we were on our way-- oops.

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:23.260
Can we turn that one back?

00:40:23.260 --> 00:40:25.960
Somebody apparently-- I had to
give the number of that picture

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:28.168
to somebody the other night
and I apparently didn't--

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:32.830
Great.

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:35.350
Thank you.

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:37.180
At this point, I just
want to summarize,

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:40.150
with this picture, some of
the historical significance

00:40:40.150 --> 00:40:41.890
of our activities.

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:45.160
I've already mentioned to you
that in the history of science

00:40:45.160 --> 00:40:47.290
we developed a first
order understanding

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:51.280
of a small planet, chemically
very much like our own,

00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:55.210
with many geological
relationships

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:56.650
to our own planet.

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:59.570
But this was done now by
Apollo for the first time.

00:40:59.570 --> 00:41:01.900
And it's a very
significant and detailed

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:04.390
scientific understanding
of that planet,

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:06.370
and has implications
for our understanding

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:07.900
of our own planet.

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.740
Particularly, its early history.

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:15.130
And particularly, the origins
of life on this planet.

00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:20.620
The astronaut, and in this case,
yours truly with Gene Cernan

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:23.320
reflected in my
visor, symbolizes

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:25.580
for, in the history
of technology,

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:28.840
one of the, if not the
greatest, explosions of know-how

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:31.150
that technology has seen.

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:34.910
And although done in
a spirit, if you will,

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:36.820
of competition with the
former Soviet Union,

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:40.600
nevertheless was done without
a direct threat of war.

00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:46.000
And that technology base
continues to benefit us today

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.070
in many, many different ways.

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:51.190
The flag of the United
States symbolizes not only

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:55.210
what the country did in
and of itself in response

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:57.400
to the challenge
that was perceived

00:41:57.400 --> 00:41:59.890
at the time from the
former Soviet Union,

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:03.040
but also represents, really,
what free men and women can

00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:07.995
do when faced with a challenge
that they believe in,

00:42:07.995 --> 00:42:09.940
and believe the meeting
of that challenge

00:42:09.940 --> 00:42:12.770
is the most important thing
they can do with their lives.

00:42:12.770 --> 00:42:15.760
I'm sure you all have heard,
and maybe even use the phrase,

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:17.860
well, if you can go to
the Moon, why can't you,

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:19.680
and then you fill in the blank.

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:21.910
Well, the point is that
you can do whatever

00:42:21.910 --> 00:42:23.990
you put into that blank, if--

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:26.980
it's a big if-- if you can
motivate young men and women

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:30.820
like yourselves to believe
that that's the most important

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:34.550
they can do with their
lives at that point in time.

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:35.980
It may turn out
that later on they

00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:39.340
have second feelings about
it, but nevertheless,

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:40.990
if the belief is
there at the time,

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:42.265
you can do almost anything.

00:42:42.265 --> 00:42:44.860
In fact, I don't know
what is beyond us if we

00:42:44.860 --> 00:42:46.840
have those kind of beliefs.

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:49.450
And finally, in the history
of the human species,

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:52.420
symbolized by the Earth
above the flag, and more

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:56.470
appropriately, maybe symbolized
by this view of the Earth

00:42:56.470 --> 00:43:00.400
rising from behind the Moon.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:04.660
We now have both the
technological and psychological

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:09.410
awareness, I think, to move
the human species into space.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.560
There are resources
that can serve

00:43:11.560 --> 00:43:15.160
the environment of
the Earth on the Moon,

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:18.130
particularly as we search in
the not too distant future

00:43:18.130 --> 00:43:23.360
for that necessary
alternative to fossil fuels.

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.900
There are the resources,
additional resources,

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:30.670
on the Moon that can make human
enclaves there self-sufficient.

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:32.990
The same applies to Mars.

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:35.230
And if we have any
kind of long term,

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:37.660
and I mean long term
species survival instinct,

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:41.770
then the enclaves of the human
species on those two planets

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:44.300
are going to be extraordinarily
important to us,

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:48.520
as well as what they do to
enable the Earth, indeed,

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:50.280
to survive itself.

00:43:50.280 --> 00:43:53.610
And so Apollo has given
us that new opportunity.

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:56.710
What we do with that, literally,
new evolutionary status

00:43:56.710 --> 00:44:00.380
is probably very difficult
to say at this point.

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:05.410
But nevertheless, as a result
of the efforts of Apollo,

00:44:05.410 --> 00:44:08.810
including some people
in this room here today,

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:11.620
many people in this
room here today,

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:15.550
we now have options that we
never had before and wouldn't

00:44:15.550 --> 00:44:17.090
have without it.

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:23.530
Now, let's digress, as we walk
off the stage here, and digress

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:29.050
a bit to the science by itself.

00:44:29.050 --> 00:44:33.750
Before Apollo, and at the end
of the so-called unmanned,

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:37.180
literally, automated
lunar exploration program,

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:42.010
we had a general understanding
of the evolution of the Moon.

00:44:42.010 --> 00:44:44.770
That there was a very
old and highly cratered

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:50.710
crust, that it had been modified
by a large circular impact

00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:56.420
basins, some more
circular than others.

00:44:56.420 --> 00:45:00.040
The Highland basins were filled
with some light-colored smooth

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:04.390
planes we didn't understand, and
by the way, still don't today.

00:45:04.390 --> 00:45:08.020
There were almost
basaltic lava flows

00:45:08.020 --> 00:45:11.770
in most of the basins on
the near side of the Moon.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:13.870
There were additional
volcanic deposits

00:45:13.870 --> 00:45:16.340
that looked like they had
been pyroclastic, that is,

00:45:16.340 --> 00:45:18.520
the kind of fire fountains
that we see today

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:22.090
on the island of Hawaii and
elsewhere around the planet.

00:45:22.090 --> 00:45:25.480
And that there was this deep
layer of pulverized debris,

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:27.400
the regolith,
covering all of this

00:45:27.400 --> 00:45:32.050
that had developed through
some unknown period of time

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:34.660
since these other
events occurred.

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:38.960
Now, after Apollo, we added
a great deal of detail,

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:45.490
particularly we understand
now the timeframe in which

00:45:45.490 --> 00:45:47.800
these things had happened.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:51.640
The Moon basically began within
the limits of measurement error

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:53.500
at the same time
the Earth began,

00:45:53.500 --> 00:45:55.450
about four and 1/2
billion years ago, almost

00:45:55.450 --> 00:45:57.580
4.6 billion years ago.

00:45:57.580 --> 00:46:00.070
And that is now taken
with the meteorite data

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:03.610
as the age of the
solar system itself.

00:46:03.610 --> 00:46:05.740
Early in its history,
right at the beginning,

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:08.320
near the beginning,
there was a magma ocean.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:14.260
Literally the outer, probably
500 kilometers of the Moon

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:19.240
was molten, or largely molten,
so that it differentiated.

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:22.630
That is, as it crystallized,
light crystals floated

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:25.150
and heavy crystals sank.

00:46:25.150 --> 00:46:28.060
And so you ended up with
a crust made up largely

00:46:28.060 --> 00:46:30.880
of a light mineral called
anorthositic feldspar,

00:46:30.880 --> 00:46:33.790
anorthosite is what
the rock name is used.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:36.670
And underneath that is
a mantle of the Moon

00:46:36.670 --> 00:46:41.080
that is composed largely
of olivine and pyroxene.

00:46:41.080 --> 00:46:42.970
And anytime I talk
about this now,

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:44.620
you have to put
in your own minds,

00:46:44.620 --> 00:46:46.430
was that happening on the Earth?

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:49.010
The answer is almost
certainly yes.

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:51.970
That these events were
reflected on comparable events

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:55.300
on the Earth, the Earth
being modified by its ability

00:46:55.300 --> 00:47:00.140
to withhold a fluid sphere,
whereas the Moon could not.

00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:03.430
But nevertheless,
we now have to think

00:47:03.430 --> 00:47:06.040
in terms of these events
or comparable events

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:08.260
having occurred on
the Earth as well.

00:47:08.260 --> 00:47:11.440
Following the solidification
of the magma ocean,

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:14.410
after maybe 100
million years or less,

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:19.300
the highlands begin
to retain the evidence

00:47:19.300 --> 00:47:21.790
of the intense cratering
that was going on.

00:47:21.790 --> 00:47:24.820
Indeed, the highland
areas of the Moon

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:28.240
are saturated with craters
at least 80 kilometers

00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:28.840
in diameter.

00:47:28.840 --> 00:47:30.910
That is, mathematically
saturated

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:32.590
with craters of that size.

00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:35.200
An unbelievable
period of violence

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:39.050
in the waning stages of the
formation of the solar system.

00:47:39.050 --> 00:47:41.500
And then at some point,
probably beginning

00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:47.370
about 4.2 billion years ago,
large basins began to form.

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:51.460
The old large basins
are the irregular ones,

00:47:51.460 --> 00:47:55.180
and the newer ones, the
younger ones-- still very old,

00:47:55.180 --> 00:47:57.860
3.9 billion years old or so--

00:47:57.860 --> 00:47:58.780
are circular.

00:47:58.780 --> 00:48:01.960
And that implies in itself that
during that period of time,

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:04.060
the crust was
somehow strengthened.

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:07.270
That is an area of
debate now of how

00:48:07.270 --> 00:48:09.370
did that crustal
strengthening occur,

00:48:09.370 --> 00:48:14.470
but there has been
isostatic, that is,

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:19.517
compensation of the
gravity anomalies

00:48:19.517 --> 00:48:21.850
that would have been associated
with these large basins.

00:48:21.850 --> 00:48:27.100
There has not been compensation
of the younger basins.

00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:29.900
So something strengthened
the crust at that point,

00:48:29.900 --> 00:48:32.570
so those anomalies
could last for three,

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:35.710
almost four billion years.

00:48:35.710 --> 00:48:38.980
In this period of
time, the extrusion

00:48:38.980 --> 00:48:43.240
of the basaltic lavas in
the Maria reached its peak

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:46.930
and continued for at least a
billion years-- we don't know

00:48:46.930 --> 00:48:48.940
exactly how long that occurred.

00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:50.950
And then after that things
pretty well stopped.

00:48:50.950 --> 00:48:53.043
If you had been around
three billion years ago

00:48:53.043 --> 00:48:54.460
and been able to
look at the Moon,

00:48:54.460 --> 00:48:58.138
you would have seen
roughly what we see today.

00:48:58.138 --> 00:48:59.680
There would have
been bright craters.

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:02.138
They would have been different
than the ones you see today,

00:49:02.138 --> 00:49:04.400
but nevertheless, they
would have been there.

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:06.340
And during that
three billion years,

00:49:06.340 --> 00:49:09.010
this mature regolith
surface developed,

00:49:09.010 --> 00:49:13.810
and began to accumulate solar
wind volatiles, and mostly

00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:18.580
hydrogen, quite a bit of helium,
and then, of course, as part

00:49:18.580 --> 00:49:20.680
of the helium, the Helium 3.

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:24.100
That is of some interest to us
now from an energy perspective

00:49:24.100 --> 00:49:27.450
and an environmental
perspective.

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:29.380
We, of course, also
understand a great deal

00:49:29.380 --> 00:49:31.780
about the interior of
the Moon, as a result

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:33.010
of the seismic experiments.

00:49:33.010 --> 00:49:37.110
We know roughly it's
layered character,

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:40.000
and from other constraints,
pretty much what

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:41.270
it's composed of today.

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:44.620
So again, we did learn
a tremendous amount

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:48.490
about the Moon and its history
and the history the Earth

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:49.790
as a consequence.

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:51.650
I think I'm going
to leave it there.

00:49:51.650 --> 00:49:53.317
If I could have the
lights, and if there

00:49:53.317 --> 00:49:56.346
is time for some questions,
I'll be happy to take those.

00:49:56.346 --> 00:49:57.370
And thank you very much.

00:49:57.370 --> 00:50:05.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:07.300
Do we have time for questions?

00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:08.592
AUDIENCE: Yes, we certainly do.

00:50:08.592 --> 00:50:10.785
SCHMITT: okay.

00:50:10.785 --> 00:50:11.763
Questions?

00:50:15.675 --> 00:50:17.470
Yes.

00:50:17.470 --> 00:50:20.540
AUDIENCE: After completing
[INAUDIBLE] book

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:23.380
on his fellow
astronaut, I was really

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:26.990
struck by his epilogue,
in which he talked

00:50:26.990 --> 00:50:30.600
about how the astronauts
reflected on their journeys,

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:32.425
with their own wide
variation in that

00:50:32.425 --> 00:50:34.960
and what they've
done since then.

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:39.880
I'm curious if it
does or it does not

00:50:39.880 --> 00:50:43.430
invoke certain emotions for you
when you look up in the sky,

00:50:43.430 --> 00:50:48.880
and how you've been able
to move on since then

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:51.950
and find the challenges in life.

00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:54.170
SCHMITT: Well, the first
question, does it invoke--

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:56.720
when I see it in the sky,
does it evoke emotions?

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:58.880
I must, because I
notice it more than I

00:50:58.880 --> 00:51:00.080
remember noticing the Moon.

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:03.290
I mean, it catches my eye,
and that's the best I can say.

00:51:03.290 --> 00:51:05.090
If I go around a
building and suddenly I

00:51:05.090 --> 00:51:07.370
see the Moon, where
I'm sure prior

00:51:07.370 --> 00:51:10.260
to being involved in the
Apollo program I didn't see it,

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:13.250
or didn't notice
it nearly as much.

00:51:13.250 --> 00:51:16.430
Or in the Monument
Valley, suddenly it's

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:22.010
there by the side of one of the
mesas, and I just notice it.

00:51:22.010 --> 00:51:27.110
But as far as any major change
as a result of actually going

00:51:27.110 --> 00:51:29.240
to the Moon, I have
to say I don't think

00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:31.490
there was a change in my--

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:34.910
But the maturing
experience of 10 years--

00:51:34.910 --> 00:51:37.630
actually, more closer to eight
years-- of direct involvement

00:51:37.630 --> 00:51:41.690
in a environment
in which everyone

00:51:41.690 --> 00:51:45.620
was totally dedicated to success
of this particular project,

00:51:45.620 --> 00:51:50.600
that had a tremendous
influence on, not only my life,

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:52.640
but my perspectives of
what human beings are

00:51:52.640 --> 00:51:56.040
capable of doing, as I indicated
earlier, when challenged

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:59.630
and when they believe
in the challenge.

00:51:59.630 --> 00:52:02.750
I had an advantage over many
of the other astronauts,

00:52:02.750 --> 00:52:04.550
although the vast
majority of them

00:52:04.550 --> 00:52:08.510
went on to very
challenging careers,

00:52:08.510 --> 00:52:13.050
primarily in business, but
in some other arenas as well.

00:52:13.050 --> 00:52:18.200
I also had my science,
and part of my transition

00:52:18.200 --> 00:52:21.620
was based on working
on the lunar samples

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:24.710
and trying to
synthesize, which I still

00:52:24.710 --> 00:52:30.320
spend some time on today, the
results of the Apollo Science

00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:31.700
Program.

00:52:31.700 --> 00:52:33.750
So that kept me moving.

00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:36.890
At the same time,
some old friends

00:52:36.890 --> 00:52:40.400
of Bob Siemens and mine,
George Lowe and Jim Fletcher

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:41.970
asked me if--

00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:43.730
and I may have
stimulated the question,

00:52:43.730 --> 00:52:45.230
but nevertheless,
they asked me if I

00:52:45.230 --> 00:52:48.440
would come to NASA
headquarters to work

00:52:48.440 --> 00:52:51.530
on the transfer of
NASA technologies

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:54.780
into energy-related activities.

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:57.140
You see, this all was
occurring about the same time

00:52:57.140 --> 00:53:01.950
as the first Arab
oil embargo in 1973.

00:53:01.950 --> 00:53:04.550
And it occurred to
all of us that there

00:53:04.550 --> 00:53:07.790
might be a great
deal of application

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:10.250
for NASA technologies,
Apollo technologies,

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:11.250
in the energy field.

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:13.250
And indeed, I think
there were, and there

00:53:13.250 --> 00:53:15.710
have been, maybe not as much
as I had hoped at the time.

00:53:15.710 --> 00:53:18.080
But I worked on that
for a couple years.

00:53:18.080 --> 00:53:20.600
And I had always had
in the back of my mind,

00:53:20.600 --> 00:53:22.670
from the time I was a
graduate student, really,

00:53:22.670 --> 00:53:27.200
at Harvard in the 1960s,
that getting into politics

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:29.990
was something that I probably
would look at very seriously,

00:53:29.990 --> 00:53:31.580
and I began to look
at that seriously

00:53:31.580 --> 00:53:34.850
while I was in
Washington in '74, '75,

00:53:34.850 --> 00:53:39.050
and decided to go back to
New Mexico and my home state

00:53:39.050 --> 00:53:41.575
and run for the senate.

00:53:41.575 --> 00:53:42.950
There was never
any time in there

00:53:42.950 --> 00:53:46.760
to sit back and say, gee,
what am I going to do next?

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:48.510
There was always
something on the horizon.

00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:50.180
And I just would
have to say to you

00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:54.403
that no matter what goals
you set for yourself,

00:53:54.403 --> 00:53:55.820
and I think some
of the astronauts

00:53:55.820 --> 00:53:58.070
fell victim to
this, no matter what

00:53:58.070 --> 00:54:00.320
goals you set for yourself,
keep them in context

00:54:00.320 --> 00:54:02.240
with your whole life.

00:54:02.240 --> 00:54:05.163
If it's the only thing you
have as a goal in your life

00:54:05.163 --> 00:54:06.830
and you're not thinking
about the future

00:54:06.830 --> 00:54:10.310
at all beyond that, then whether
you achieve it or don't achieve

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:13.003
it, you're going to
be at sea for a while.

00:54:13.003 --> 00:54:14.420
And I think some
of the astronauts

00:54:14.420 --> 00:54:18.926
discovered that was the case
after they'd been to the Moon.

00:54:18.926 --> 00:54:23.150
AUDIENCE: Jack, I had a
question about the post Apollo

00:54:23.150 --> 00:54:24.080
model of the Moon.

00:54:24.080 --> 00:54:27.545
There was an article in
Scientific American this summer

00:54:27.545 --> 00:54:33.380
you probably saw called "The
Apollo Legacy," Science Legacy,

00:54:33.380 --> 00:54:36.470
and it laid out the
evidence that said,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:39.470
now we have confidence
that the origin of the Moon

00:54:39.470 --> 00:54:44.090
was the result of a major
comet impact on the Earth which

00:54:44.090 --> 00:54:46.510
released this large mass
that became the Moon.

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:49.400
And this was followed by a flood
of letters in the preceding

00:54:49.400 --> 00:54:52.880
months, and the months that
followed it disputing that.

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:54.217
Do you have any thoughts as to--

00:54:54.217 --> 00:54:56.300
SCHMITT: I wish I had
written one of those letters

00:54:56.300 --> 00:54:59.865
because I dispute it as well.

00:54:59.865 --> 00:55:02.060
You can perform some
fantastic computer

00:55:02.060 --> 00:55:07.340
models that make that
a plausible hypothesis.

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:10.820
Unfortunately, I think there
is at least one major bit

00:55:10.820 --> 00:55:14.390
of geological evidence that that
model has a very difficult time

00:55:14.390 --> 00:55:17.755
explaining, and it has to
do with the orange soil

00:55:17.755 --> 00:55:19.130
that I mentioned
briefly earlier.

00:55:19.130 --> 00:55:25.700
The orange soil was volcanic
material, pyroclastic-like,

00:55:25.700 --> 00:55:28.370
the fire fountains from Hawaii.

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:32.540
There is strong geologic
and experimental evidence

00:55:32.540 --> 00:55:34.640
utilizing the orange
soil that that

00:55:34.640 --> 00:55:38.240
came from deep within the Moon.

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:41.570
Some components of it came
from below that 500 kilometers

00:55:41.570 --> 00:55:42.700
of lunar melting.

00:55:42.700 --> 00:55:45.850
And it indicates that
below that depth,

00:55:45.850 --> 00:55:48.140
the Moon is largely
undifferentiated.

00:55:48.140 --> 00:55:51.080
That is, it's
primordial material.

00:55:51.080 --> 00:55:55.790
For example, the lead,
the isotopic composition

00:55:55.790 --> 00:55:58.590
that's associated
with that orange soil,

00:55:58.590 --> 00:56:00.200
and it comes from
those depths is

00:56:00.200 --> 00:56:02.600
what we call parentless lead.

00:56:02.600 --> 00:56:04.250
I mean, it's primordial lead.

00:56:04.250 --> 00:56:07.640
It doesn't have a great
radioactive component to it,

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:10.070
as you would expect
from other things.

00:56:10.070 --> 00:56:12.090
It also has volatile
elements that

00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:15.680
are depleted in this
part of the Moon

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:19.430
that was molten at
one time, and they're

00:56:19.430 --> 00:56:22.670
concentrated in the
gases that apparently

00:56:22.670 --> 00:56:24.860
drove this volcanic eruption.

00:56:24.860 --> 00:56:26.545
And it's very difficult--

00:56:26.545 --> 00:56:27.920
I won't go into
all the details--

00:56:27.920 --> 00:56:30.680
but it's very difficult
for me, and I have not

00:56:30.680 --> 00:56:33.290
seen a rational explanation
from the proponents

00:56:33.290 --> 00:56:37.070
of the Mars-sized asteroid
theory of how you explain

00:56:37.070 --> 00:56:39.350
having a relatively
undifferentiated interior

00:56:39.350 --> 00:56:41.720
of the Moon if it
was knocked off

00:56:41.720 --> 00:56:44.370
a largely differentiated Earth.

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:46.700
I just don't know
how that works.

00:56:46.700 --> 00:56:48.950
So I am a skeptic of that model.

00:56:48.950 --> 00:56:49.642
It's a good one.

00:56:49.642 --> 00:56:51.350
It stimulated an awful
lot of discussion.

00:56:51.350 --> 00:56:54.680
That's what's fun about science
is developing the models

00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:55.970
and seeing if they work.

00:56:55.970 --> 00:56:58.370
And more often than
not, they don't.

00:56:58.370 --> 00:57:00.200
And you go on to the next one.

00:57:00.200 --> 00:57:01.880
In this case, I
think that we still

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:04.010
have some explaining to do.

00:57:04.010 --> 00:57:07.610
And so I tend to favor, if you
say, okay, what's your idea,

00:57:07.610 --> 00:57:12.200
I think that it's probably
a capture phenomenon that

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:15.860
when all the evidence is in that
we'll find that the Moon was

00:57:15.860 --> 00:57:18.680
derived in the
vicinity of the Earth,

00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:23.390
because of its overall
silicate-dominated composition,

00:57:23.390 --> 00:57:27.350
but that it was in a
separate solar orbit,

00:57:27.350 --> 00:57:30.320
but one close enough in
Earth-crossing orbit,

00:57:30.320 --> 00:57:31.610
and eventually was captured.

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:35.140
And it turns out, that
although in the early days,

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:38.600
the calculations indicated
that was a very, very, very

00:57:38.600 --> 00:57:41.360
mathematically, a
very special case,

00:57:41.360 --> 00:57:44.620
it now does not look like
it's quite so special.

00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:47.642
The work on capture
by a few people

00:57:47.642 --> 00:57:49.350
has now indicated that
there are probably

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:51.308
a number of different
ways in which it could be

00:57:51.308 --> 00:57:53.690
captured into an Earth orbit.

00:57:53.690 --> 00:57:57.260
So that's where I'm leaning
right now is in that direction.

00:57:57.260 --> 00:57:59.030
It helps to explain
a lot of things

00:57:59.030 --> 00:58:02.510
that I don't think the
Mars-sized asteroid will

00:58:02.510 --> 00:58:05.290
explain.

00:58:05.290 --> 00:58:05.910
Yes, sir.

00:58:05.910 --> 00:58:08.050
AUDIENCE: What were your
plans during the mission

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:11.990
if there were a
solar particle event?

00:58:11.990 --> 00:58:15.530
SCHMITT: Depending on the level
of the solar particle event,

00:58:15.530 --> 00:58:19.250
we would try to put
as much mass as we

00:58:19.250 --> 00:58:20.960
could between us and the event.

00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:23.660
That was the only plan we had.

00:58:23.660 --> 00:58:27.290
We knew that that was
one of the major risks

00:58:27.290 --> 00:58:30.760
to the health and safety
of the Apollo crews,

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:34.820
and there was not any
planning beyond that.

00:58:34.820 --> 00:58:36.830
Although there was
extensive planning

00:58:36.830 --> 00:58:39.560
to how to observe a flare.

00:58:39.560 --> 00:58:43.040
Where on the sun and
a flare occurring

00:58:43.040 --> 00:58:46.580
would affect the
vicinity of the Earth,

00:58:46.580 --> 00:58:51.770
trying to predict how
much time would you have.

00:58:51.770 --> 00:58:56.720
And that was worked in
to the contingency plans.

00:58:56.720 --> 00:59:00.120
Based on what part of the
mission the event occurred,

00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:02.420
you would then move to get
as much mass between you

00:59:02.420 --> 00:59:04.430
and that event as possible.

00:59:04.430 --> 00:59:07.370
And ultimately meant you'd
get into the command module

00:59:07.370 --> 00:59:10.495
as fast as you could, because
that would have the most mass.

00:59:10.495 --> 00:59:11.870
And so if we were
on the surface,

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:14.930
we would try to get off the
surface as soon as we possibly

00:59:14.930 --> 00:59:16.910
could and rendezvous
with the command module.

00:59:16.910 --> 00:59:19.493
AUDIENCE: How much warning
did you feel you had with--

00:59:19.493 --> 00:59:21.410
SCHMITT: There is still
some debate over that.

00:59:21.410 --> 00:59:25.080
At the time, it was felt
that it was about an hour.

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.950
Although some people who
still study that problem

00:59:27.950 --> 00:59:30.440
suggest that it may be
only about 30 minutes.

00:59:30.440 --> 00:59:33.740
And I can't tell you why there's
that factor of two discrepancy.

00:59:33.740 --> 00:59:35.520
They've not been able
to explain it to me,

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:37.600
and I can't explain it to you.

00:59:37.600 --> 00:59:39.830
But there is that kind of
a debate going on about it.

00:59:39.830 --> 00:59:40.940
But it's not much now.

00:59:40.940 --> 00:59:44.150
The only one I know
now, there probably

00:59:44.150 --> 00:59:48.890
was a very easy solution to the
lunar surface flare problem,

00:59:48.890 --> 00:59:52.160
and that is to use a
linear-shaped charge,

00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:55.970
explosive charge,
to create a trench,

00:59:55.970 --> 00:59:59.810
drive the lunar Rover over
it, and have some capability

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:04.310
to at least pile dirt, pile
regolith, on top of that Rover.

01:00:04.310 --> 01:00:07.730
That would be a very good way
to deal with the mass issue,

01:00:07.730 --> 01:00:12.260
and just live off--

01:00:12.260 --> 01:00:14.840
by cutting down the rate
at which you're working,

01:00:14.840 --> 01:00:16.640
you could live off
your consumables

01:00:16.640 --> 01:00:20.180
for a number of hours longer
under that Rover than you would

01:00:20.180 --> 01:00:21.620
of if you were working.

01:00:21.620 --> 01:00:23.780
Now, you could
augment that solution

01:00:23.780 --> 01:00:27.380
by having some additional
consumables on the Rover

01:00:27.380 --> 01:00:30.470
that you then could attach
to, with a vacuum transfer,

01:00:30.470 --> 01:00:31.615
into your suit.

01:00:31.615 --> 01:00:32.990
All of those things
are feasible.

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:35.323
And as a matter of fact, as
we look towards lunar bases,

01:00:35.323 --> 01:00:37.010
I personally think
that's probably

01:00:37.010 --> 01:00:41.150
the way you'll deal with
that continued hazard

01:00:41.150 --> 01:00:42.800
and the risk
associated with it, is

01:00:42.800 --> 01:00:45.980
to have those kinds of
systems worked out in advance.

01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:47.750
So I don't think
in the long run it

01:00:47.750 --> 01:00:54.090
needs to be a major issue for,
actually, permanent activities

01:00:54.090 --> 01:00:54.590
on the Moon.

01:00:58.250 --> 01:01:02.500
I'll take your question and
then I'll have a closing one.

01:01:02.500 --> 01:01:05.230
AUDIENCE: What are your
thoughts about the issues

01:01:05.230 --> 01:01:09.630
of manned versus unmanned in
lunar and planetary missions?

01:01:09.630 --> 01:01:13.240
I know the American
Astronomical Society--

01:01:13.240 --> 01:01:15.660
members of the American
Astronomical Society

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:19.996
are very much in favor
of the unmanned missions,

01:01:19.996 --> 01:01:21.827
that they are much
more cost effective.

01:01:21.827 --> 01:01:23.660
SCHMITT: Well, I'm in
favor of both of them.

01:01:23.660 --> 01:01:27.010
I just was a
principal investigator

01:01:27.010 --> 01:01:28.780
on a proposal for
the Discovery Program

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:33.160
to put a lunar Rover, a
new Rover, automated Rover,

01:01:33.160 --> 01:01:34.732
on the surface of
the Moon to get

01:01:34.732 --> 01:01:36.940
a better three-dimensional
understanding of the lunar

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:39.430
regolith, scientific
understanding of the regolith,

01:01:39.430 --> 01:01:42.430
not only for science
in many different ways,

01:01:42.430 --> 01:01:44.170
but also to better
understand how

01:01:44.170 --> 01:01:49.600
to design future habitats,
machines, and things like that,

01:01:49.600 --> 01:01:52.510
that would operate on
and within the regolith.

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:55.970
So I feel very strongly that you
need to find the right balance.

01:01:55.970 --> 01:02:01.540
But at some point, you'll always
find me feeling very strongly

01:02:01.540 --> 01:02:06.100
that the integration of
robotic or automated technology

01:02:06.100 --> 01:02:08.710
and the human
capabilities is ultimately

01:02:08.710 --> 01:02:12.280
the way to get the greatest
return on the buck spent.

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:19.100
It's partly my nature.

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:22.460
I hope it's partly a good
logical position to be in.

01:02:22.460 --> 01:02:26.830
I just don't see how we
can fully scientifically

01:02:26.830 --> 01:02:30.370
or humanistically take advantage
of the opportunities that

01:02:30.370 --> 01:02:33.430
are available to us in space
without the combination,

01:02:33.430 --> 01:02:36.040
and particularly, without
the presence of human beings

01:02:36.040 --> 01:02:38.830
at some point in that pyramid.

01:02:38.830 --> 01:02:45.790
The argument will go on.

01:02:45.790 --> 01:02:48.910
I have heard people, including
my good friend Carl Sagan,

01:02:48.910 --> 01:02:51.040
say that everything
we learned on Apollo

01:02:51.040 --> 01:02:53.360
could have been learned
with an automated system.

01:02:53.360 --> 01:02:57.250
I'll give him that, but he's
never had to cost that out.

01:02:57.250 --> 01:02:59.290
And I just have a
strong gut feeling

01:02:59.290 --> 01:03:02.110
as an Earth scientist that to
get that level of understanding

01:03:02.110 --> 01:03:04.963
of the history of a
small planet purely

01:03:04.963 --> 01:03:06.880
through automated missions
would have cost you

01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:10.030
at least as much in then
year dollars as the Apollo

01:03:10.030 --> 01:03:10.852
program cost.

01:03:10.852 --> 01:03:12.310
And I'm not sure
it would have been

01:03:12.310 --> 01:03:15.160
possible with the technologies
that were available then.

01:03:15.160 --> 01:03:17.860
Now we have a somewhat
different picture.

01:03:17.860 --> 01:03:21.190
What you can gain with some
of the advanced imaging

01:03:21.190 --> 01:03:24.400
and analytical mass
spectrometry, particularly

01:03:24.400 --> 01:03:26.470
technologies that we
could put on a Rover,

01:03:26.470 --> 01:03:29.020
automated Rover on the
Moon, is far and above

01:03:29.020 --> 01:03:31.540
what we could have
done 25 years ago.

01:03:31.540 --> 01:03:33.940
But still, I think
the argument's

01:03:33.940 --> 01:03:36.610
a useful one because it
makes everybody think.

01:03:36.610 --> 01:03:40.120
But the other side, shall I
say, if I'm on the man side,

01:03:40.120 --> 01:03:43.240
has never really had to
cost out all of these things

01:03:43.240 --> 01:03:48.820
that a human being can
do when they're left--

01:03:48.820 --> 01:03:50.890
a trained and
experienced human being

01:03:50.890 --> 01:03:54.198
can do when they're put into
their own environment in space.

01:03:54.198 --> 01:03:56.740
MODERATOR: You asked the closing
question I was going to ask.

01:03:56.740 --> 01:03:59.020
So with that, I will thank
Jack Schmitt for a lecture

01:03:59.020 --> 01:04:00.910
that we'll remember
perhaps until we

01:04:00.910 --> 01:04:02.290
see the next man on the Moon.

01:04:02.290 --> 01:04:02.610
Thank you.

01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:03.360
SCHMITT: Pleasure.

01:04:03.360 --> 01:04:04.760
[APPLAUSE]

